the many saints of newark the sopranos  prequel from writer producer david chase  and director alan taylor has finally  been released the movie follows the  complicated life of mobster dickie  moltesanti including his relationship  with young tony soprano and so far it  has proven a little divisive among  longtime fans of the landmark hbo series  sadly this probably isn't the kind of  buzz warner brothers was hoping for  after finally returning to david chase's  popular mob universe the movie is also  the latest experimental offering in the  dual release strategy warner brothers  has been deploying throughout the  pandemic some of the biggest most  anticipated releases distributed by the  studio have been given simultaneous day  and date debuts in theaters and on the  hbo max streaming service thus far the  experiment has been a mixed bag at least  as far as box office returns are  concerned some of these films have had  successful theatrical runs that haven't  been entirely cannibalized by their hbo  max releases including godzilla vs kong  and immortal combat but not all of  warner's films have been so successful  and the many states of newark has landed  squarely in the latter category oh my  god oh jesus  the many states of new york pulled in a  paltry five million dollars at the box  office in its first weekend of release  an underperformance even by the  diminished standards of pandemic era box  office pro projected before the weekend  that many saints would grow somewhere in  the range of 7 million to 12 million  dollars so why has this happened well  there's no smoking gun for why the  sopranos prequel didn't kill it at the  box office but it seems likely that a  combination of factors may have worked  together to reduce the movie's box  office takings according to a recent  analysis by deadline the main reason  could be that the target audience for  the many saints of newark is an older  demographic one that has been slow to  return to theaters over coronavirus  spheres the most successful films of the  pandemic era have been tentpole films  that appeal to younger viewers such as  black widow which grossed 80.8 million  dollars on its opening weekend  certainly it would be interesting to see  data on how many saints performed on hbo  max enough people may have watched it on  the streaming service for warner  brothers to be happy with it and hbo  just inked a massive new overall deal  with david chase 2 so they definitely  seem bullish on the potential for future  sopranos content nobody can really say  for sure whether the movie was a total  flop until those hbo figures are  released the other reason why the movie  might not be crushing at the box office  could be word of mouth there are a lot  of young soprano fans out there but they  didn't show up either the movie has a  mediocre c plus audience rating from  cinemascore and has gotten a lukewarm  response from sopranos fans on social  media if that's the case then any  sopranos fans who do want to check out  the new movie might do so on the  streaming service they already pay for  instead of shelling out for movie  tickets more than anything else however  there's a 250 pound symbiote-shaped  elephant in the room here a massive  rival to the many states of newark that  undeniably sucked up the box office  oxygen on this balmy october weekend  there's gonna be carnage  the many states of newark landed in  theaters and on hbo max on october 1st  the same day the woody harrelson and tom  hardy flick venom let there be carnage  got its release amid much fan  anticipation notably let there be  carnage did not receive a day and date  streaming release from sony pictures one  of the major studios most reluctant to  embrace the dual release strategy  preferred by warner brothers in disney  in the end sony's gamble on a pandemic  weekend at the theater paid off  dethroning black widow to become the  highest grossing theatrical release of  the pandemic era with so many moviegoers  flocking to see hardy's venom square off  against harrelson's carnage it seems  like there just wasn't much market share  left for many saints to claim  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]  you
 

